# JoyHub Registry

Use this skill when you need to work with a JoyHub (SkillHub-compatible) registry: search skills, inspect metadata, install a package, or publish a new version.

> Important: Prefer the official JoyHub CLI (`@toolnets/joyhub-cli@0.2.0`, bin `joyhub`) for registry workflows. Official agent skills `find-skills` and `share-skill` call this CLI. Do not copy tokens by hand. The ClawHub-compatible API and `/.well-known/clawhub.json` remain available for OpenClaw clients.

Pin every CLI invocation. Do not use unconstrained `latest`:

```bash
npx --yes --package=@toolnets/joyhub-cli@0.2.0 joyhub <command>
```

## What The Registry Is

JoyHub is an enterprise-oriented skill registry based on SkillHub architecture. It stores versioned skill packages, supports namespace-based skill management, and keeps `SKILL.md` compatibility with OpenSkills-style packages.

Key facts:

- Internal coordinates use `@{namespace}/{skill_slug}`.
- The official CLI accepts `@namespace/skill_slug`, `namespace/skill_slug`, and the compatible `namespace--skill_slug` form.
- ClawHub-compatible clients use a `{namespace}--{skill_slug}` slug.
- `latest` always means the latest published version, never draft or pending review.
- Search is authenticated. Unauthenticated search returns `401`; do not fall back to anonymous search.
- If no namespace is specified, it defaults to `@global`.
- `{skill_slug}` can be used instead of `global--{skill_slug}`
- Visible search results match the current user's JoyHub Web visibility (platform role and namespace RBAC).

## Configure The CLI

Point `joyhub` at the registry base URL:

```bash
export JOYHUB_REGISTRY=${SKILLHUB_PUBLIC_BASE_URL}
```

Alternatively, use the `--registry` parameter every time, for example:

```bash
npx --yes --package=@toolnets/joyhub-cli@0.2.0 \
  joyhub install my-skill --registry ${SKILLHUB_PUBLIC_BASE_URL}
```

On first use, bind the CLI with Device Flow. Do not ask the user to paste a token:

```bash
npx --yes --package=@toolnets/joyhub-cli@0.2.0 joyhub auth ensure --json
```

Credentials are stored in `~/.joyhub/credentials.json` (mode `0600`), isolated by registry URL. Tokens must not appear in stdout, logs, or follow-up prompts.

Optional local check:

```bash
curl ${SKILLHUB_PUBLIC_BASE_URL}/.well-known/clawhub.json
```

Expected response:

```json
{"apiBase":"/api/v1"}
```

## Coordinate Rules - IMPORTANT

The registry supports these naming forms:

| Registry coordinate | Canonical slug for ClawHub-compatible clients |
|---|---|
| `@global/my-skill` | `my-skill` |
| `@team-name/my-skill` | `team-name--my-skill` |

Rules:

- `--` is the namespace separator in the compatibility layer.
- If there is no `--`, the skill is treated as `@global/...`.
- `latest` resolves to the latest published version only.

Examples:

```bash
npx --yes --package=@toolnets/joyhub-cli@0.2.0 joyhub install my-skill
npx --yes --package=@toolnets/joyhub-cli@0.2.0 joyhub install my-skill --version 1.2.0
npx --yes --package=@toolnets/joyhub-cli@0.2.0 joyhub install @team-name/my-skill
```

## Common Workflows

Run `joyhub auth ensure --json` before search, install, or publish.

### Search (`find-skills`)

```bash
npx --yes --package=@toolnets/joyhub-cli@0.2.0 \
  joyhub search --query "email" --limit 10 --json
```

Use an empty query when you want a broad listing of skills the current user can see:

```bash
npx --yes --package=@toolnets/joyhub-cli@0.2.0 \
  joyhub search --query "" --limit 50 --json
```

Install only after the user chooses an exact coordinate. Do not auto-install from a fuzzy search.

### Install

```bash
npx --yes --package=@toolnets/joyhub-cli@0.2.0 joyhub install my-skill
npx --yes --package=@toolnets/joyhub-cli@0.2.0 joyhub install my-skill --version 1.2.0
npx --yes --package=@toolnets/joyhub-cli@0.2.0 joyhub install @team-name/my-skill
```

### Publish (`share-skill`)

Prepare a skill package directory, dry-run, then publish only after the user confirms the namespace and version:

```bash
npx --yes --package=@toolnets/joyhub-cli@0.2.0 \
  joyhub publish ./my-skill --namespace my-team --dry-run --json
npx --yes --package=@toolnets/joyhub-cli@0.2.0 \
  joyhub publish ./my-skill --namespace my-team --json
```

Publishing requires authentication and sufficient permissions in the target namespace.

## Authentication And Visibility

CLI search always requires a valid login. The server returns skills the current user is allowed to see.

- `NAMESPACE_ONLY`: authenticated namespace members only.
- `PRIVATE`: owner or explicitly authorized users only.
- Publish, star, and other write operations always require authentication.

If a request fails with `401`, run `joyhub auth ensure --json` and retry. If a request fails with `403`, check:

- whether the skill belongs to a team namespace,
- whether the skill is `NAMESPACE_ONLY` or `PRIVATE`,
- whether the login is valid,
- whether you have namespace publish permissions.

## Skill Package Contract

The registry expects OpenSkills-style packages with `SKILL.md` as the entry point.

## Publishing Guidance

Just need to follow the OpenSkills-style standards.
